# Extension activation and public surface

An editor extension becomes part of a larger application. It cannot assume that its
module is always running, that every workspace is trusted, or that every registered
feature should act on every Markdown document. It must tell the host what users can
see, then create and dispose the runtime objects that make those declarations true.

This produces two related surfaces. The manifest is declarative: it names commands,
views, settings, preview resources, and keybindings before the extension runs.
Activation is operational: it creates the repository, providers, URI handling, and
command implementations for the current editor session.

## Declare discoverability before behavior

The manifest lets the editor show a feature without loading its implementation just
to learn that the feature exists. Literate declares:

- a fragment view in the Explorer and a refresh action in that view's title;
- commands for processing, fragment authoring, language-server restart, and
  publication export;
- settings for mapped-language behavior, configured server processes, and
  publication tools;
- a keybinding that can cancel a pending automatic completion request;
- participation in the Markdown parser and locally packaged browser scripts for
  diagrams and inline exploration.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Manifest[Extension manifest] --> Commands[Command palette and enablement]
    Manifest --> View[Fragment Explorer contribution]
    Manifest --> Settings[Configuration schema and scope]
    Manifest --> Markdown[Markdown parser and preview resources]
    Manifest --> Keys[Context-sensitive keybinding]
    Commands --> Activate[Host activates the extension when needed]
    View --> Activate
    Markdown --> Activate
    Activate --> Runtime[Session-owned adapters and providers]
```

The arrows from the manifest mean “makes discoverable to the host.” They do not
execute commands or create providers. The final arrow marks the point at which the
extension module can allocate runtime state. Explicit activation-event duplication
is unnecessary for this contribution-driven surface; the host can infer when the
contributed capabilities require activation.

The public command identifiers and setting keys are compatibility boundaries. Their
human-readable titles and descriptions may improve, but changing an identifier can
break keybindings, tasks, menus, or user configuration outside the repository.

## Activation assembles services in dependency order

Activation first creates the editor-side repository because fragment providers,
preview rendering, and command handlers depend on its snapshots. The repository and
its diagnostic collection enter the extension context's subscription set so the host
can dispose them together.

Publication commands and mapped language features are registered around narrower
interfaces rather than receiving unrestricted access to repository internals.
Publication receives a serialization boundary so its capture and helper lifetime do
not overlap ordinary generation. Language features receive a source-capture function
that returns documents and templates without generating files.

The initial repository pass is awaited and read-only. This gives navigation and
preview a discovered project without writing source or HTML merely because the
extension became active. Failure to read one generation input does not prevent
independent commands from being registered; later operations can report their own
specific errors.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Host as VS Code
    participant Entry as Extension entry point
    participant Repo as Project repository
    participant Surface as Commands, views, and providers
    participant Preview as Markdown integration

    Host->>Entry: Activate with extension context
    Entry->>Repo: Create session-owned repository
    Entry->>Surface: Register publication and language boundaries
    Entry->>Repo: Capture initial project state without writes
    Repo-->>Entry: Initial snapshot or contained failure
    Entry->>Surface: Register fragment commands, tree, and symbol providers
    Entry->>Preview: Create navigation handler and return parser extension
    Entry-->>Host: Activated public Markdown contribution
```

The diagram emphasizes dependency order, not source-file order. Some registrations
can be implemented in separate modules, and browser scripts are loaded by the
Markdown host rather than called during this sequence. The key invariant is that no
provider observes a half-constructed shared repository.

The exported `activate` function and returned `extendMarkdownIt` hook are the runtime
entry points recognized by VS Code. Literate does not expose its repository as a
general extension API. Other modules within the bundle share it through explicit
construction and registration.

## Registration is broad; action is narrow

Fragment tools currently register against Markdown because `.literate` documents
use the built-in Markdown language support. That selector is intentionally broader
than the semantic domain. Each provider must therefore locate a current workspace
folder and refresh the relevant project. Navigation and refactoring decline when
the cursor does not identify a valid fragment occurrence. Fragment-name completion
uses the broader lexical signal of an unmatched `<<` before the cursor.

Mapped programming-language features narrow further: they require an open
file-backed `.literate` document in a current local workspace, an accepted fragment
body, a configured canonical language, and a position that maps into generated code.
A registration tells VS Code who may answer a request; it does not guarantee that
every request receives a result.

This fail-quiet behavior matters in mixed Markdown workspaces. Semantic hovers,
navigation, refactoring, and mapped language services decline outside their domain.
Fragment-name completion is intentionally less semantic and may appear after an
unmatched `<<`; accepting a suggestion still edits only the current cursor range.

## Commands express different effect levels

The public command surface groups actions that look similar in a palette but have
different authority:

- **Process** captures projects, asks the shared engine for complete plans, updates
  diagnostics and navigation, and commits valid requested outputs through the
  transaction boundary.
- **Create fragment** and **Split fragment** edit the active literate document at an
  explicitly chosen position. Normal change processing follows those edits.
- **Refresh Fragment Explorer** invalidates the view so visible children are requested
  again; it does not itself generate files.
- **Restart language servers** disposes configured sessions so later requests create
  fresh ones.
- **Export LaTeX** and **Export PDF** begin explicit publication workflows with fresh
  input capture, user choices, and isolated external processes.

Keeping these meanings distinct is part of the public contract. A view refresh must
not become Process, activation must not become export, and asking for completion must
not write outputs.

## Trust is a set of capability boundaries

Workspace Trust is not best modeled as a single switch that makes the entire
extension disappear. Reading Markdown and identifying fragment symbols are different
risks from launching a configured executable or accepting a browser-to-editor
navigation capability.

Literate applies trust at the boundary that needs authority:

- preview cross-document capabilities are minted and honored only for trusted,
  currently present workspaces;
- mapped language servers require a trusted local file workspace and configuration
  taken from the user's global settings;
- publication requires a trusted local desktop workspace and performs additional
  path, process, and platform checks;
- ordinary symbol inspection remains useful without starting an external process.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Request[Requested extension capability] --> Process{Starts an external process?}
    Process -->|Yes| Trusted{Trusted local workspace and allowed configuration?}
    Trusted -->|No| Deny[Decline with no process]
    Trusted -->|Yes| Start[Create bounded isolated operation]
    Process -->|No| Write{Writes project files?}
    Write -->|Yes| Commit[Preflight and commit a complete plan]
    Write -->|No| Navigate{Crosses from preview into the editor?}
    Navigate -->|Yes| Capability{Valid temporary capability in current workspace?}
    Capability -->|No| Plain[Leave text or link inert]
    Capability -->|Yes| Open[Resolve fresh logical target and open safely]
    Navigate -->|No| Inspect[Serve read-only project information]
```

The arrows classify authority, not individual command names. Filesystem generation
has its own destination preflight and transaction policy described in the host and
output chapters; trust does not replace those checks. Conversely, path checks do not
make an arbitrary external executable safe. Each boundary defends the capability it
actually grants.

## Configuration authority follows risk

A convenience preference such as automatic mapped completion can vary by resource.
Executable paths and process initialization data have a stronger authority and are
restricted to machine-scoped user configuration. Workspace content must not be able
to select a program that the extension launches.

For mapped language servers, a setting identifies an absolute executable, literal
arguments, and optional protocol configuration. No shell performs expansion. An
invalid entry is rejected rather than partially interpreted. Publication similarly
uses user-controlled tool paths while keeping project-relative resource choices,
such as a font manifest, inside separately validated workspace boundaries.

VS Code exposes these policies through the `contributes.configuration` schema and
runtime configuration inspection. The schema makes settings discoverable and
communicates scope; runtime checks remain necessary because configuration is input,
not proof.

## Browser scripts are public resources with private authority

The Mermaid renderer and exploration client run in the Markdown preview's browser
context. The manifest packages them as local preview scripts, but neither script owns
editor APIs. They receive escaped DOM containers and inert semantic data.

Navigation crosses back into the extension only through the activation-owned URI
handler. That handler validates scheme, authority, token shape, session membership,
expiry, trust, workspace identity, fresh semantic target, and canonical path before
opening a document. This narrow bridge is preferable to exposing arbitrary commands
to rendered content.

The split also improves portability. Generated HTML can use the same screen model
and exploration client with ordinary local links, while editor preview supplies
capabilities appropriate to its host.

## Disposal closes the input side first

Every listener, provider, view, timer, diagnostic collection, server coordinator,
and URI handler has a lifetime. Activation attaches their disposables to the
extension context. When the host deactivates the extension, disposal prevents new
work, cancels timers, clears bounded caches, releases subscriptions, and then drops
workspace snapshots.

Repository disposal marks the adapter closed before draining its state, so a queued
callback cannot repopulate caches during teardown. Preview navigation invalidates its
capabilities and ignores late URL-resolution results. Language sessions and
publication helpers own their own shutdown policies. Idempotent disposal is essential
because failures and normal deactivation may converge on the same cleanup path.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Declared
    Declared --> Activating: host needs a contribution
    Activating --> Active: repository and registrations ready
    Activating --> Active: nonfatal initial scan failure contained
    Active --> Disposing: host deactivates or owner shuts down
    Disposing --> Disposed: inputs closed, resources released
    Disposed --> Disposed: repeated cleanup is inert
```

The state diagram describes the extension session, not language-server subprocesses
or output transactions, which have their own finer-grained states. Notice that a
contained initial scan failure does not create a half-active public surface. Commands
and providers can still exist and report or recover from current project conditions.

## A small public surface supports a large internal architecture

The host sees commands, a view, settings, a Markdown extension, browser resources,
and provider registrations. Behind them, the pure engine, symbol collector,
publication model, projection layer, transaction coordinator, and process adapters
remain separately testable components.

This is the purpose of activation: not to contain the whole program, but to join
well-defined responsibilities at the last responsible moment. Future commands and
views should preserve that shape. Declare discoverability in the manifest, register
a narrow adapter during activation, keep semantic work in host-independent modules,
gate authority where it is exercised, and make the complete lifetime disposable.

With the editor environment assembled, the next part can turn from fragment symbols
to programming-language intelligence. That work begins with the projection problem:
the author edits scattered contributions, while a language server expects assembled
source with stable positions and project context.
